CINEMASSIVE ALPHA FX REBUILD NOTES

These are my notes from rebuilding a CineMassive Alpha FX from scratch. They document the process that worked on my system. Other Alpha FX configurations may have different hardware, card counts, or display configurations.

Before doing anything to the original installation, I strongly recommend imaging the original hard drive. My original drive had read errors and bad sectors, but it still contained software, configuration information, and other material that was extremely helpful during the rebuild.

ORIGINAL HARDWARE

The computer originally had two memory slots populated with 4GB PC3 memory modules for a total of 8GB.

There are two front-accessible drive bays. Mine had one 500GB SATA hard drive installed and the second bay was empty.

The system also has a Blu-ray drive.

HARDWARE UPGRADES

I upgraded the memory to 16GB using two 8GB PC3 memory modules.

I replaced the original 500GB SATA hard drive with a 240GB SATA SSD.

The original hard drive was preserved rather than reused for the rebuild.

INSTALL WINDOWS 7

Download a Windows 7 Ultimate ISO that includes Service Pack 1.

Use Rufus or another utility to create a bootable Windows 7 USB drive.

On my Alpha FX, the USB drive did not initially appear as a boot option.

To boot from USB:

	Start the computer and press DEL to enter the BIOS.
	Go to BOOT.
	Go to HDD Drive BBS Priorities near the bottom of the screen.
	Move the USB drive up in priority using the + key.
	Go to Save & Exit and select the option to boot from the USB drive one time.

The Windows installer should start.

Go through the normal Windows 7 installation process and select the new SSD as the installation destination.

When asked for a username use:

LocalAdmin

This isn't required, but LocalAdmin appears to be the username CineMassive used on the original system.

My Alpha FX has a serial number on the lid beginning with CMD. I used this CMD number as the Windows computer name.

Set a password and complete the Windows installation.

PREPARE THE CINEMASSIVE INSTALLATION FILES

Extract Cinemassive.zip directly to the C:\ drive.

You should end up with:

	C:\Cinemassive

My installation files contained these folders:

	C:\Cinemassive\CineNet 2.7.40
	C:\Cinemassive\Control Screen Drivers
	C:\Cinemassive\Driver Package 3.2.3

INSTALL BASIC SYSTEM DRIVERS

Before installing the CineMassive driver package, install the normal Windows hardware drivers.

IMPORTANT: The CineMassive driver installation later applies Group Policy restrictions intended to prevent its drivers from being replaced. Because of that, this is a good time to get the normal system hardware working first.

The devices I installed drivers for were:

	Intel HD Graphics 3000 - Driver located in C:\Cinemassive\Control Screen Drivers
	Intel(R) 82574L Gigabit Network Controller
	Intel(R) 82579LM Gigabit Network Controller
	Intel(R) Active Management Technology - SOL
	Intel(R) 6 Series/C200 Series Chipset Family SMBus Controller - 1C22
	Intel(R) 6 Series/C200 Series Management Engine Interface - 1C3A

After installing these drivers, Device Manager still showed several Standard VGA Graphics Adapters with this hardware identification:

	PCI\VEN_1002&DEV_6763&CC_0300

There were also multiple Multimedia Video Controllers with:

	PCI\VEN_DADA&DEV_0168&CC_0400

These devices will be handled by the CineMassive driver package later.

