VERIFY WINDOWS COMPONENTS

Before installing the CineMassive software, verify that the following components are installed:

	Windows 7 Service Pack 1
	DirectX 11 - Run dxdiag to verify the installed DirectX version.
	.NET Framework 4.7.2
	Windows6.1-KB3033929-x64.msu
	Microsoft Silverlight

ENABLE WINDOWS AERO

Aero is important for CineMassive to work properly.

After the video drivers are installed, right-click the Windows desktop and select Personalize.

Select one of the Windows Aero themes.

If Aero does not work, Windows provides a troubleshooting link for diagnosing Aero problems.

A simple indication that Aero is functioning is that the Windows title bars become transparent.

WINDOWS ACTIVATION

Windows should be activated before continuing with the final CineMassive configuration.
I found that having Windows properly activated helped avoid problems with the completed environment.

REBOOT

Reboot the computer.

Log back into Windows as LocalAdmin.

INSTALL THE CINEMASSIVE DRIVER PACKAGE

Go to:

C:\Cinemassive\Driver Package 3.2.3

Right-click INSTALL.EXE and select Run as Administrator.

Click Next.

The installer should detect the CineMassive hardware installed in the system.

On my system it displayed:

	CineView GFX-4 G3 V1.6.1
	CineView IEX V7.14.1

I left the detected devices checked and clicked Next.

The next screen is important.

The selections made here define the basic display arrangement and how the CineMassive driver presents the display wall to Windows.

Although these settings are apparently changeable later, I did not experiment with changing them after installation.

For my 2x2 display wall I selected:

	4 screens
	2 screens per GPU
	2x2 arrangement

The next screen defines the individual display resolution.

I selected:

1920 x 1080 per display

With four 1920x1080 displays arranged as 2x2, the installer calculated a total Windows desktop area of:

	3840 x 2160

Select the appropriate refresh rate and color depth for your displays. I kept the defaults.

There is also an option labeled:

Force Driver Install

I do not know exactly what this option changes internally. During my rebuild I enabled Force Driver Install and the installation completed successfully.

Click Next to configure the system and install the drivers.

During installation, Windows may display a driver security prompt.

Check:

Always trust software from...

Then click Install.

I also received a warning involving the Intel HD Graphics 3000 during this process. This did not prevent my system from working after the installation.

When installation finishes:

REBOOT THE COMPUTER.

INSTALL CINENET

My CineNet installation files are located in:

C:\Cinemassive\CineNet 2.7.40

I installed the CineNet components using msiexec from an Administrator command prompt.

Install CineNet Live System Manager:

msiexec /i "Install CineNet Live System Manager (4).msi"

Install CineNet Live Alpha Control Service:

msiexec /i "Install CineNet Live Alpha Control Service.msi"

Install CineNet Live Client:

msiexec /i "Install CineNet Live Client (4).msi"

CURRENT DOCUMENTATION STATUS

These notes document the process I used to get Windows, the system hardware, the CineMassive driver package, and CineNet installed on my Alpha FX.

The original CineMassive environment appears to have relied on software, drivers, configuration information, and documentation that are now difficult to locate publicly.

Because different Alpha FX systems may have been configured differently, hardware IDs, card counts, driver versions, network settings, and display layouts should be compared against the original system whenever possible.

If you have an original Alpha FX drive, preserve and image it before attempting a rebuild.
